--[[
    Fix Stale Stash IDs: stop Anomaly attaching loot and a map marker to an object
    that is not a stash. Such a marker can never be opened and never goes away.
    Also repairs saves that already carry one.

    Every line number here is vanilla Anomaly 1.5.3, from tools\_unpacked\scripts.
    A mod that overrides one of these files will renumber it; find the function by
    name instead.

    The defect, all of it stock:
      treasure_manager holds its candidate pool in `caches`, keyed by server object
      id. on_game_load (:626) builds it once by walking ids 1..65534 and enrolling
      every object IsInvbox accepts (:640), guarded by `if (caches_count > 0) then
      return end`. An available box is stored as `false` (:643; the comment at :299
      says so outright).

      release_stash_by_id (:602-607) guards on the value, not the key:

          function release_stash_by_id(id)
              if id and caches[id] then       -- false for every available box
                  caches[id] = nil
                  caches_count = caches_count - 1
              end
          end

      So the one thing it exists to remove is the one thing it declines to touch.
      Its single caller is game_setup.script:411-419, which releases every object
      named in new_game_setup.ltx [remove_objects] and calls the pruner for the
      inventory boxes among them. Three of those 146 entries are boxes, so every
      campaign starts with three entries pointing at ids the engine has already
      taken back.

      Ordering makes that certain rather than occasional. load_state runs from the
      engine's CALifeStorageManager_load, before on_game_load is sent from
      bind_stalker_ext:61, so caches_count is already positive on a load and the
      walk only ever happens on a new game. game_setup then releases the three
      boxes on actor_on_first_update and the prune no-ops.

      The engine treats an id as a slot handle, not an identity:
      xrServer::entity_Destroy returns it to a free pool (xrServer.cpp:873 ->
      id_generator.h:106) and it is reissued later, so a stale entry eventually
      names a live object that is not a box. Reuse is not uniform: the generator is
      256 blocks of 256 ids and takes from the block with the oldest free timestamp
      (xrServer.h:144-155, id_generator.h:35-38,101), which is why this is rare
      rather than constant. A box cannot be destroyed any other way: CInventoryBox
      is a plain CGameObject with no CPHDestroyable or CDamageManager
      (InventoryBox.h:6), so only an explicit script release frees one.

      Nothing downstream checks class. get_random_stash self-heals an id whose
      object is *gone* (:320-325), which is why the bug needs a recycled id rather
      than merely a released one, but its class filter is opt-in on inv_box
      (:288-296) and almost no caller sets it. set_random_stash checks nothing at
      all: it fetches the object only to read m_game_vertex_id for tiering, then
      writes the contents string and adds the map spot. Once a marker lands on a
      non-box it is permanent, because try_spawn_treasure (:512) only runs from
      physic_object_on_use_callback, gated on IsInvbox (:708-711).

      Matching stash *names* cannot substitute for the class test. An id recycled
      onto a cooking kit does not carry the name of the box that used to own it,
      and a name list cannot notice that an id changed hands.

    What this does. One callback and three wrappers, all on treasure_manager. The
    numbers are used in the comments at each install site:

        (1) the sweep, on actor_on_first_update
        (2) treasure_manager.get_random_stash
        (3) treasure_manager.set_random_stash
        (4) treasure_manager.release_stash_by_id

      (1) Walks the pool once per session and drops every entry whose id is no
          longer an inventory box, clearing any bogus map spot with it. An entry
          that was holding loot has its contents and marker moved onto a real box
          first, preferring the level the bogus marker was on. This is what
          repairs an existing save.

          Relocation runs before pruning, and `swept` is set on entry so a failure
          never retries: pruning first would leave a contents string deleted from
          caches and held only in a local. Candidates are screened against the
          doomed set explicitly, so a bogus id cannot become another's
          replacement. The move writes caches directly rather than re-entering
          set_random_stash, so contents are not re-rolled and no news fires; the
          hint is rebuilt from the new box's own strings, which costs a relocated
          treasure_unique its bespoke text.

      (2) Validates the id base drew. A bad draw is dropped and redrawn with
          inv_box forced on, so a caller can never receive a non-box. Validating
          after the fact rather than forcing the filter on every call matters:
          base's filter block runs before the cheap `v == false` test (:288-299),
          so it pays alife_object + clsid + name for the whole pool including the
          filled entries the next line discards. Checking the one id that came
          back is a single lookup. Re-calling is safe because get_random_stash has
          nothing to undo: it sets a file-local, may prune a dead id, and returns
          strings. The map spot is attached later, by set_random_stash.

      (3) Refuses an id that is not a box before it can attach loot or a marker,
          and drops the entry. This is what covers the reserve-now-apply-later
          callers, which pick an id and commit it much later. It also refuses a
          real box whose m_game_vertex_id would not survive the graph read at
          :372, which is guarded against a missing object but not a bad vertex;
          that refusal keeps the entry, because the box belongs in the pool.

      (4) Makes the pruner able to prune. Promoting a present-but-`false` value to
          `true` before delegating is the whole fix: the vanilla body then removes
          the key and decrements its own file-local caches_count, which no wrapper
          can do directly.

          This is the only hook that changes behaviour on a well-formed call: one
          that is a silent no-op in vanilla now removes the entry, which is what
          every caller is asking for, game_setup's "Clear inventory boxes from
          their manager" included. (2) and (3) diverge from base only when handed
          an id that is not a box, which is exactly where base produced the bug.

          It earns its place over leaving this to (1) because of ordering. Both
          run on actor_on_first_update and axr_main stores intercepts as
          intercepts[name][fn] = true (axr_main.script:249-261), a table keyed by
          the function object, so their order is unspecified. If (1) runs first on
          a new game it reports a clean pool and the entries game_setup then seeds
          survive the session. Fixing the pruner removes the entry at the moment
          of release instead. No map spot is cleared here, unlike drop(): the case
          this newly enables is a `false` entry, and treasure_manager only raises a
          spot when it writes contents.

    Why there is no server_entity_on_unregister hook:
      Pruning on unregister is the tidy root fix and is not safe from a mod. The
      callback that would reach an inventory box is the se_* script class:
      CInventoryBox binds to se_item.se_invbox, whose on_unregister sends it
      (se_item.script:390-392), and CALifeObjectRegistry's destructor calls
      on_unregister on every object it holds (alife_object_registry.cpp:17-27). A
      hook there would empty the pool during teardown, and any save written after
      that would persist an empty pool. That trades an occasional bogus stash for
      every stash in the game. (The other sender, alife_release, never fires for a
      box: _ALIFE_UNREGISTER lists only outfit, device, PDA, food and attachment
      classes, class_registrator.script:137-145.)

    Not fixed here, on purpose:
      A refusal at (3) does not substitute a fresh stash, so that reward is not
      issued. Nothing obtainable is lost, since the alternative was a marker on an
      object that cannot be opened, but on the PDA contact path the miss is
      permanent: ui_pda_npc_tab discards the return value and sets
      stash_read = true regardless (:696-701). Only (1) relocates loot, and only
      (1) can, because it acts on damage that already exists, where a contents
      string is already sitting in the pool. (3) is handed an id whose reward does
      not exist yet. Both point-of-use paths print the contents before erasing them, so a
      discarded stash is recoverable by hand from the log.

      A bad game vertex on a real box is only screened at (3). get_random_stash
      dereferences the drawn object's m_game_vertex_id bare at :328, and
      box_in_valid_map does the same for every candidate at :597, both before (2)
      sees a return value. Such an entry is a genuine box and belongs in the pool,
      so the sweep leaves it; zzz_stashfix_debug's `audit` counts them separately
      as bad-vertex.
--]]


local SPOTS = { "treasure", "treasure_searched", "treasure_unique" }

local swept = false
local hooked = false
local last_report = nil


-- Debug flags, read from axr_options.ltx. Both default to on, so a normal
-- install never touches them and nothing here changes for a player who does not
-- know they exist. zzz_stashfix_debug's F7 console writes them; they live in the
-- user config rather than in the save because they have to be in effect *before*
-- the load they apply to, and because the point of them is to compare the same
-- save with the fix on and off.
--
-- axr_main.config is constructed at file scope (axr_main.script:15), so it is
-- readable this early. r_value's third argument is the type, where 1 is bool.
--
--   autosweep = false   pool repair on load is skipped; the point-of-use hooks
--                       stay on. This is what makes the audit useful: without
--                       it the sweep has already run by the time you can open
--                       the F7 console, and the pool is always clean.
--   enabled   = false   the whole mod is inert. No hooks, no sweep. For
--                       measuring a save as it would be without this installed.
local FLAGS = "stashfix"

local function flag(key)
    if not (axr_main and axr_main.config) then
        return true
    end
    return axr_main.config:r_value(FLAGS, key, 1, true) ~= false
end


-- alife_object logs a full callstack for a nil or out-of-range id
-- (_g.script:2062-2067), so screen ids before they reach it.
local function usable_id(id)
    return (type(id) == "number") and (id >= 0) and (id < 65535)
end


-- Object-taking cores, and thin id-taking wrappers over them.
--
-- Re-fetching the same server object is the most repeated cost in this file. Every
-- loop below needs two or three facts about one entry, and a test that fetched for
-- itself would cost three alife_object calls to screen a single relocation
-- candidate. alife_object is a Lua wrapper whose only job is the
-- bounds guard usable_id has already done, and it reads and calls the alife()
-- global on every invocation (_g.script:2062-2068), so each fetch is a Lua call
-- plus two luabind crossings.
--
-- So the loops hoist alife() once, fetch once with sim:object(id), and pass the
-- object to the obj_* functions. The id-taking wrappers stay for the point-of-use
-- hooks, which handle one id per call and where a fetch is not worth threading.
--
-- IsInvbox is kept rather than inlined to `clsid() ~= clsid.inventory_box_s`. It
-- covers both the _s and client variants (_g.script:2943-2948) and is what
-- treasure_manager builds the pool with, so the two agree by construction; the
-- one Lua call it costs is noise next to the luabind clsid() crossing inside it.
local function obj_is_box(se_obj)
    return (se_obj and IsInvbox(nil, se_obj:clsid())) and true or false
end


-- True only if this ID currently belongs to an inventory box. Deliberately the
-- same test treasure_manager builds the pool with, so the two agree. For a
-- server object clsid() is always the _s variant, so this matches
-- get_random_stash's stricter `~= clsid.inventory_box_s` exactly.
local function is_stash(id)
    if not usable_id(id) then
        return false
    end
    return obj_is_box(alife_object(id))
end


-- Describe an ID for the log. Stale entries are the interesting case, so name
-- what the ID actually is now rather than just reporting a number.
local function describe_obj(id, se_obj)
    if not usable_id(id) then
        return "id " .. tostring(id) .. " (not a usable id)"
    end
    if not se_obj then
        return "id " .. tostring(id) .. " (no object)"
    end
    local owner = se_obj.parent_id and (se_obj.parent_id ~= 65535) and alife_object(se_obj.parent_id)
    return "id " .. tostring(id) .. " (" .. tostring(se_obj:section_name())
        .. (owner and (" inside " .. tostring(owner:section_name())) or "") .. ")"
end


local function describe(id)
    return describe_obj(id, usable_id(id) and alife_object(id) or nil)
end


-- Which treasure spot, if any, is currently attached to this ID.
-- level.map_* take a u16 (level_script.cpp:412,428,474), so screen the id here
-- too rather than relying on every caller having done it.
local function current_spot(id)
    if not usable_id(id) then
        return nil
    end
    for i = 1, #SPOTS do
        if level.map_has_object_spot(id, SPOTS[i]) ~= 0 then
            return SPOTS[i]
        end
    end
end


-- CGameGraph::vertex is unchecked pointer arithmetic, `return m_nodes +
-- vertex_id` (game_graph_inline.h:117-120), with no VERIFY of its own at all;
-- the nearest ones guard accessible() (:86,:92), and valid_vertex_id is right
-- there at :96-98 exported to Lua (game_graph_script.cpp:67) and simply not
-- used. treasure_manager gets away with calling it bare because it believes
-- every ID it holds is a live box; we call it on IDs we already know are
-- something else, whose m_game_vertex_id may be stale or the 65535 sentinel, so
-- the read has to be bounds-checked first.
local function obj_level(se_obj)
    local gvid = se_obj and se_obj.m_game_vertex_id
    if not gvid then
        return nil
    end
    local gg = game_graph()
    if not gg:valid_vertex_id(gvid) then
        return nil
    end
    return alife():level_name(gg:vertex(gvid):level_id())
end


local function stash_level(id)
    if not usable_id(id) then
        return nil
    end
    return obj_level(alife_object(id))
end


-- Marker hint for a box, built the way treasure_manager builds it: from the box's
-- own st_<name>_name / _descr strings, with level_prefix
-- substituted. Falls back to "", which is what treasure_manager passes when the
-- translations are missing, leaving the spot's default hint in place.
local function stash_hint(id)
    if not usable_id(id) then
        return ""
    end
    local se_obj = alife_object(id)
    if not se_obj then
        return ""
    end

    local name = se_obj:name()
    local lvl = obj_level(se_obj)
    if lvl and string.find(name, "level_prefix") then
        name = string.gsub(name, "level_prefix", lvl, 1)
    end

    local name_key, descr_key = "st_" .. name .. "_name", "st_" .. name .. "_descr"
    local name_str = game.translate_string(name_key)
    local descr_str = game.translate_string(descr_key)
    if (name_str ~= name_key) and (descr_str ~= descr_key) then
        return "%c[255,255,160,0]" .. name_str .. "\\n%c[default]" .. descr_str
    end
    return ""
end


-- Every empty, genuinely-still-a-box entry that orphaned loot could be moved
-- into, with the level each one sits on. An empty entry that is still a box, plus
-- treasure_manager's own blacklisted-map filter. base applies that filter only when
-- no_spot is falsy (:299, `no_spot or box_in_valid_map(id)`); applying it
-- unconditionally here is deliberately stricter, so a relocated treasure_unique
-- will not land on a blacklisted map even though it could have started on one.
--
-- box_in_valid_map does the same unchecked game_graph():vertex() read that
-- stash_level guards against (:597), so a candidate whose
-- m_game_vertex_id is stale or the 65535 sentinel must be dropped *before* it is
-- offered to that function. A nil from stash_level is exactly that test, and
-- the level it returns is the one the same-level preference needs anyway.
--
-- The by-name blacklist get_random_stash applies when inv_box is set
-- (caches_blacklist, :410) cannot be mirrored: it is a file-local. on_game_load
-- already excludes blacklisted names when building the pool (:642), so the only
-- exposure is an entry inherited from a save made before a name was
-- blacklisted.
--
-- Built once per sweep rather than once per orphan. Screening a candidate costs an
-- alife_object lookup plus two graph reads, so a per-orphan rescan of the
-- ~1200-entry pool put five figures of luabind calls on the frame that runs the
-- sweep. Drawing from a prebuilt list is pure Lua.
--
-- One fetch per entry, through the hoisted sim: the class test and the level read
-- both take the object. box_in_valid_map fetches it a second time and that cannot
-- be avoided, since the blacklisted_maps table it consults is a treasure_manager
-- file-local, and knowing the level here is not enough to apply the filter. Its
-- `type(valid_map) == "function"` guard is hoisted too; it cannot change mid-loop.
local function collect_candidates(exclude)
    local tm = treasure_manager
    local valid_map = tm.box_in_valid_map
    local has_valid_map = (type(valid_map) == "function")
    local sim = alife()
    local out, n = {}, 0

    for id, state in pairs(tm.caches) do
        if (state == false) and not exclude[id] and usable_id(id) then
            local se_obj = sim:object(id)
            if obj_is_box(se_obj) then
                local lvl = obj_level(se_obj)
                if lvl and ((not has_valid_map) or valid_map(id)) then
                    n = n + 1
                    out[n] = { id = id, lvl = lvl }
                end
            end
        end
    end

    return out
end


-- Draw one candidate, preferring the level the bogus marker was sitting on so
-- the stash stays on the map the player was already heading to; falls back to
-- anywhere in the pool. Marks what it returns, so two orphans can never be
-- given the same box.
local function take_candidate(pool, want_level)
    local same, any = {}, {}

    for i = 1, #pool do
        local c = pool[i]
        if not c.taken then
            any[#any + 1] = i
            if want_level and (c.lvl == want_level) then
                same[#same + 1] = i
            end
        end
    end

    local list = (#same > 0) and same or ((#any > 0) and any)
    if not list then
        return nil
    end

    local c = pool[list[math.random(#list)]]
    c.taken = true
    return c.id
end


-- CMapManager::AddMapLocation has no duplicate guard, unlike
-- AddRelationLocation, which R_ASSERTs on one (map_manager.cpp:123-132 vs :147),
-- so the same (spot_type, id) pair can be present more than once, and
-- RemoveMapLocation erases only the first match it finds (:159-171). So raise a
-- spot only when there isn't one, and remove in a loop.
local function raise_spot(id, spot, hint)
    if not usable_id(id) then
        return
    end
    if level.map_has_object_spot(id, spot) == 0 then
        level.map_add_object_spot_ser(id, spot, hint or "")
    end
end


local function clear_spot(id, spot)
    if not usable_id(id) then
        return
    end
    for _ = 1, 8 do
        if level.map_has_object_spot(id, spot) == 0 then
            return
        end
        level.map_remove_object_spot(id, spot)
    end
end


-- Remove an entry from the pool, clearing any map spot it managed to acquire.
local function drop(id)
    local tm = treasure_manager
    local state = tm.caches[id]
    if state == nil then
        return
    end

    for i = 1, #SPOTS do
        clear_spot(id, SPOTS[i])
    end

    -- release_stash_by_id keeps treasure_manager's private caches_count in step
    -- with the table, which we cannot do ourselves, since caches_count is a
    -- file-local. It early-returns on a falsy value though, so an available
    -- (false) entry has to be made truthy before it will act on it.
    --
    -- Hook 6 wraps that function to do the same promotion, so this one is
    -- normally redundant and idempotent: the wrapper sees `true` and delegates
    -- straight through. Kept because drop() must not depend on (4) having
    -- been installed, and because promoting here costs one table write.
    if type(tm.release_stash_by_id) == "function" then
        if not state then
            tm.caches[id] = true
        end
        tm.release_stash_by_id(id)
    end

    -- Belt and braces: if release_stash_by_id was missing or replaced by
    -- something that did not remove the key, drop it directly. caches_count is
    -- then one too high, which only ever feeds `> 0` / `<= 0` tests.
    if tm.caches[id] ~= nil then
        tm.caches[id] = nil
    end
end


-- drop() as the point-of-use hooks want it: identical, except that it names any
-- contents it is about to erase.
--
-- A stale entry whose state is a string is loot the player may already have been
-- told about, and release_stash_by_id deletes the entry outright, so once it is
-- gone the string is not recoverable from anywhere. The sweep relocates that case
-- and prints the contents when it cannot; these hooks bin it (see "Not fixed
-- here, on purpose"), so printing is the only thing standing between a discarded
-- stash and no record of it at all.
--
-- Unreachable from the get_random_stash hook, where the state is always false
-- because that is the only thing base draws. Used there anyway, for one table
-- read, so that all three hooks discard the same way rather than two of them
-- doing it quietly.
local function drop_in_use(id)
    local state = treasure_manager.caches[id]
    if type(state) == "string" then
        printf("! STASHFIX| the entry being dropped was holding loot -- discarded: %s", state)
    end
    drop(id)
end


-- Revalidate the whole pool. ~1200 alife_object calls, once per session, against
-- the 65534-iteration walk treasure_manager already does on a new game.
local function do_sweep()
    if swept then
        return
    end
    swept = true

    local tm = treasure_manager
    if not (tm and tm.caches) then
        return
    end

    -- Collect before mutating; drop() writes to the table we are walking. Capture
    -- everything a bogus stash needs relocating (contents, spot type, level)
    -- while its entry and marker still exist.
    -- One fetch per entry, reused by all four facts a doomed entry needs. sim is
    -- hoisted for the same reason: alife() is a global read plus a luabind call,
    -- and this loop runs the length of the pool.
    local checked = 0
    local sim = alife()
    local doomed, orphaned = {}, {}
    for id, state in pairs(tm.caches) do
        checked = checked + 1
        local se_obj = usable_id(id) and sim:object(id) or nil
        if not obj_is_box(se_obj) then
            doomed[#doomed + 1] = id
            if type(state) == "string" then
                orphaned[#orphaned + 1] = {
                    id       = id,
                    contents = state,
                    spot     = current_spot(id),
                    lvl      = obj_level(se_obj),
                    was      = describe_obj(id, se_obj),
                }
            end
        end
    end

    -- Say so even when there is nothing to do. Otherwise a healthy save is
    -- indistinguishable from the mod not being loaded at all, which is the
    -- likelier of the two given it installs as an MO2 junction that has to be
    -- refreshed and ticked by hand.
    if #doomed == 0 then
        last_report = { checked = checked, dropped = 0, orphans = 0, moved = 0 }
        printf("~ STASHFIX| swept stash pool: %s entries checked, no stale ids", checked)
        return
    end

    local excluded = {}
    for i = 1, #doomed do
        excluded[doomed[i]] = true
    end

    -- Relocate orphaned loot into a real box rather than binning it: write the
    -- contents string across unchanged and re-raise the same spot type on the new
    -- box.
    --
    -- This runs before the stale entries are pruned, not after. A stale entry is
    -- inert either way, since nothing can draw it while this loop is running, but
    -- a contents string that has been removed from caches exists only in the local
    -- above, and `swept` was set on entry, so an error anywhere in here would take
    -- it with it and never retry. Candidates are screened against `excluded`
    -- rather than by having already been removed from the table, so a bogus id
    -- still cannot be chosen as another's replacement.
    local moved = 0
    local candidates = (#orphaned > 0) and collect_candidates(excluded) or nil
    for i = 1, #orphaned do
        local o = orphaned[i]
        local new_id = take_candidate(candidates, o.lvl)
        if new_id then
            tm.caches[new_id] = o.contents
            -- Only re-raise a marker if the bogus entry had one. An unmarked stash
            -- (the no_spot callers) should stay unmarked after the move.
            if o.spot then
                raise_spot(new_id, o.spot, stash_hint(new_id))
            end
            moved = moved + 1
            printf("~ STASHFIX| moved orphaned stash loot: %s -> %s%s", o.was,
                describe(new_id), o.spot and "" or " (no marker, was unmarked)")
        else
            printf("! STASHFIX| no valid box left to move loot from %s -- discarded: %s",
                o.was, o.contents)
        end
    end

    for i = 1, #doomed do
        drop(doomed[i])
    end

    last_report = { checked = checked, dropped = #doomed, orphans = #orphaned, moved = moved }

    printf("~ STASHFIX| swept stash pool: %s entries checked, dropped %s stale %s, relocated %s of %s bogus %s",
        checked, #doomed, (#doomed == 1) and "entry" or "entries",
        moved, #orphaned, (#orphaned == 1) and "stash" or "stashes")
end


-- What actor_on_first_update actually calls. Separated from do_sweep so the
-- autosweep flag suppresses the automatic run without also disabling a manual
-- one. `swept` is deliberately left clear here, so the pool is untouched and
-- still sweepable rather than marked done.
local function sweep_on_load()
    if not flag("autosweep") then
        printf("! STASHFIX| autosweep is OFF (debug flag in axr_options.ltx [%s]): "
            .. "pool left unrepaired. Run 'stashfix sweep' in the F7 console, or "
            .. "'stashfix autosweep on' and reload to restore normal behaviour.", FLAGS)
        return
    end
    do_sweep()
end


-- Debug entry points, used by zzz_stashfix_debug's F7 console command. Nothing
-- in normal play calls any of them.
--
-- Clearing `swept` from outside is safe because do_sweep sets it again on entry,
-- so a forced run is still one-shot with respect to itself. It bypasses the
-- autosweep flag on purpose: that flag exists to defer the sweep until you ask
-- for it, and this is the asking.
function debug_force_sweep()
    -- Refuse while the mod is flagged off. Sweeping would repair the pool the
    -- flag exists to leave alone, and it would do it with the point-of-use hooks
    -- absent, a state that never occurs in a real install, so anything measured
    -- from it would be measuring nothing.
    if not flag("enabled") then
        return false, "the fix is flagged off (enabled=false) -- 'stashfix fix on' and reload first"
    end
    swept = false
    do_sweep()
    return true
end


function debug_state()
    return {
        hooked = hooked,
        swept = swept,
        enabled = flag("enabled"),
        autosweep = flag("autosweep"),
        -- What the last sweep did, so a session that repaired itself normally
        -- can still report it after the fact. The audit alone would just see
        -- the clean pool that was left behind.
        last = last_report,
    }
end


-- Wrapping happens in on_game_start rather than at file scope so it lands on top
-- of anything that patched treasure_manager at load time. Mods that do so exist,
-- though in practice they patch set_random_stash, try_spawn_treasure or
-- init_settings rather than get_random_stash.
--
-- axr_main.on_game_start loads every script in its first loop before calling any
-- on_game_start in its second (axr_main.script:307-327), so every file-scope
-- wrapper is already in place by the time this runs. The loading is not
-- something that loop does explicitly: it only reads _G[file_name] to test for
-- an on_game_start. That read is what loads the script: _G carries a lazy-load
-- __index which calls process_file_if_exists on a miss (script_engine.cpp
-- :348,356). Outermost is where the validation has to be.
--
-- The name is not what secures that. on_game_start dispatch is alphabetical by
-- filename and ~60 enabled scripts sort after zzz_aaa_. None of them touch
-- treasure_manager today, so this is outermost in practice, but the prefix buys
-- uniqueness rather than lastness.
function on_game_start()
    -- Debug kill switch, checked before anything else is touched: no wrappers,
    -- no sweep callback, nothing. This is how you measure a save as it would be
    -- without the mod, without unticking it in MO2 and losing the F7 console
    -- along with it. Loud, because an inert fix that looks installed is exactly
    -- the failure this line exists to make impossible to miss.
    if not flag("enabled") then
        printf("! STASHFIX| DISABLED by debug flag in axr_options.ltx [%s] enabled=false "
            .. "-- no hooks, no sweep. 'stashfix fix on' in the F7 console + reload to re-enable.", FLAGS)
        return
    end

    local tm = treasure_manager
    if not (tm and tm.caches and tm.get_random_stash and tm.set_random_stash) then
        printf("! STASHFIX| inactive: treasure_manager is missing or has an unexpected shape")
        return
    end

    -- Normally the Lua state is rebuilt on every load. CALifeSimulator's ctor
    -- runs restart_all(), which calls script_engine().init()
    -- (alife_simulator.cpp:28-48), so these locals reset and on_game_start
    -- wraps exactly once. The -keep_lua launch flag skips that (:30), leaving a
    -- persistent state where on_game_start runs again: without these two guards
    -- the wrappers would stack a layer per load and the sweep would never run a
    -- second time.
    --
    -- Registration sits outside the once-only block on purpose. It does not need
    -- to be repeated, since a persistent Lua state keeps the registry too, but
    -- axr_main.callback_set stores intercepts[name][fn] = true (axr_main.script
    -- :249-261), a set keyed by the function object, so re-registering the same
    -- upvalue is a no-op. Doing it unconditionally makes the callback's presence
    -- independent of whether the state was rebuilt, rather than inferred from it.
    -- This is the only registration in the file; there is nothing to repeat at
    -- the end of it.
    RegisterScriptCallback("actor_on_first_update", sweep_on_load)

    swept = false
    if hooked then
        return
    end
    hooked = true

    -- (4) release_stash_by_id: make it able to prune the entries it is asked about.
    -- Vanilla guards on the value, `if id and caches[id]`
    -- (_unpacked/scripts/treasure_manager.script:602-607), and an available box
    -- is stored as `false` (:643), so the one thing it exists to remove is the
    -- one thing it declines to touch. Promoting to truthy first is the whole
    -- repair: the vanilla body then deletes the key and decrements its own
    -- file-local caches_count, which is the part no wrapper can do directly.
    --
    -- This is the only hook that changes behaviour another mod can see. It is
    -- what the callers already ask for, vanilla game_setup.script:411-419
    -- included, so it restores intent rather than redefining it.
    local base_release_stash_by_id = tm.release_stash_by_id
    if type(base_release_stash_by_id) == "function" then
        tm.release_stash_by_id = function(id)
            -- Only ever promote a key that is already there. Writing to an
            -- absent id would enrol a stash instead of removing one.
            if usable_id(id) and (tm.caches[id] == false) then
                tm.caches[id] = true
            end
            return base_release_stash_by_id(id)
        end
    end

    -- (2) get_random_stash: never hand a stale entry to a caller. Validate what base
    -- drew and only fall back to its own candidate filter when the draw was
    -- actually bad, rather than forcing that filter on unconditionally.
    --
    -- The filter is expensive and mostly wasted. Its block runs before the cheap
    -- `v == false` availability test (:288-299), so with inv_box on it pays
    -- alife_object + clsid() + name() for every entry in the ~1200-entry pool,
    -- including the filled ones the next line discards. That is ~3600 luabind
    -- calls per stash award, on top of the box_in_valid_map and
    -- box_in_same_map lookups the loop already does, each of which fetches the
    -- object again. Stale entries are a fraction of a percent of the pool, so
    -- nearly every one of those calls proves something we could have proved with
    -- one lookup after the fact.
    --
    -- Validating the result costs exactly one alife_object on the common path. A
    -- bad draw drops the entry and re-calls with inv_box = true, paying the full
    -- filter only when it has been earned. The guarantee is unchanged: a caller
    -- can still never receive an id that is not an inventory box.
    --
    -- Safe to re-call because get_random_stash has no side effects to undo. It
    -- sets last_secret (rewritten by the retry), prunes an id whose object is gone
    -- (:320-325), and returns strings. The map spot is attached later and
    -- separately, by set_random_stash.
    --
    -- No behaviour change on the clean path: base is called with the same
    -- arguments and its return values are handed back untouched. In particular
    -- the caches_blacklist by-name test is not lost, because it sits inside
    -- base's own `if inv_box then` branch (:288-296) and so never ran for a
    -- falsy-inv_box call in vanilla either; the redraw applies it, since that
    -- call forces inv_box on. The only difference a caller can observe is that
    -- one which would have been handed a stale id now gets whatever the strict
    -- redraw finds, possibly nil.
    local base_get_random_stash = tm.get_random_stash
    tm.get_random_stash = function(no_spot, hint, spawn_local, inv_box)
        -- A caller that asks for the filter itself gets it, unchanged and
        -- unvalidated: it already guarantees a box.
        if inv_box then
            return base_get_random_stash(no_spot, hint, spawn_local, inv_box)
        end

        local id, a, b, c = base_get_random_stash(no_spot, hint, spawn_local, inv_box)
        if (id == nil) or is_stash(id) then
            return id, a, b, c
        end

        printf("! STASHFIX| stash draw landed on a non-box: %s -- dropping it and redrawing", describe(id))
        if usable_id(id) and tm.caches[id] ~= nil then
            drop_in_use(id)
        end
        return base_get_random_stash(no_spot, hint, spawn_local, true)
    end

    -- (3) set_random_stash: refuse to attach loot or a marker to an ID that is not
    -- a box any more.
    -- Returning nil is what set_random_stash itself returns when it assigns
    -- nothing, so callers that check the result already handle this.
    local base_set_random_stash = tm.set_random_stash
    tm.set_random_stash = function(no_spot, hint, bonus_items, id, dbg)
        -- The debug simulation passes synthetic counter values, not real IDs.
        if not dbg then
            -- Both checks below want the same server object, so fetch it once.
            local se_obj = usable_id(id) and alife_object(id) or nil

            if not obj_is_box(se_obj) then
                printf("! STASHFIX| refused to place a stash on a non-box: %s", describe_obj(id, se_obj))
                if usable_id(id) and tm.caches[id] ~= nil then
                    drop_in_use(id)
                end
                return
            end

            -- A real box is not automatically a safe argument. set_random_stash
            -- indexes the game graph with se_obj.m_game_vertex_id for level
            -- tiering (:372). That read is guarded against a missing object but
            -- not against a bad vertex, so a box whose vertex is stale or the
            -- 65535 sentinel is an out-of-bounds read in there. Same guard the
            -- relocation candidates get, for the same reason. No drop: the entry
            -- is a genuine inventory box and belongs in the pool.
            if not obj_level(se_obj) then
                printf("! STASHFIX| refused a box with an unusable game vertex: %s", describe_obj(id, se_obj))
                return
            end
        end
        return base_set_random_stash(no_spot, hint, bonus_items, id, dbg)
    end
end
